.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_REMOVE 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_remove \- deregister a selectable from an event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_event_loop_remove(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function deregisters reg. It is safe to call from inside a
callback for the registration that you remove (self-removal on an
error is a common pattern), from any other thread, and at the same
time as an in-flight dispatch for the same registration.

No later callback for this registration ever runs after this call
returns. This covers a callback that the loop already collected but
that has not started yet, such as one that waits in the queue of a
dispatch worker thread with num_reactor_threads > 1. This call does
not, however, wait for a dispatch that is already in flight at the
moment of the call.

A dispatch that is in flight can use whatever arg points to, so a
caller that cannot otherwise know when that memory is safe to free
needs handlers.on_removed (see ccol_event_loop_add(3)). It fires
exactly one time, asynchronously, and only after no dispatch of this
registration, and no other ccol_event_loop call that resolves it, can
be in progress. A caller that is sure that no dispatch can be in flight
at the moment of the removal may free arg at once, with no on_removed;
self-removal from inside the callback of that exact registration is
one such case, and a registration that the loop never dispatched is
another.

This function does not close an fd and does not touch the lifetime of
a queue; it releases only the registration bookkeeping of the
ccol_event_loop. This mirrors the "the caller owns the fd" contract of
ccol_selectable_from_fd.

Remove every registration of an fd before you close that fd, as
libevent and libuv also require. Once the last of them is removed, the
loop has stopped watching the fd by the time this call returns, and
the fd number is free. A new registration of the same fd, or of a new
descriptor that reuses the number, is independent of the removed one:
nothing that the loop does afterwards for the removed registration (a
dispatch in flight, or the later release of its bookkeeping) changes
how the new one is watched. A registration whose fd is closed first
holds the number until it is removed, and in the meantime a
ccol_event_loop_add(3) of a descriptor that reuses the number fails
with ccol_not_permitted for the direction that the stale registration
holds, and can fail with ccol_unexpected_failure for the other
direction.

For a queue selectable or a ccol_channel selectable, the loop finishes
its own bookkeeping for that queue before this call returns: it
unlinks the waiter of the registration from the queue and releases the
registration bookkeeping. A dispatch that is already in flight is a
separate matter, because its callback holds the selectable that the
loop gave it and reaches the queue through that selectable. Unlike arg
above, the queue needs no on_removed handshake for this, because a
destroy of the queue waits for any such callback to finish with it
(see ccol_circular_queue_destroy(3)). The ordinary sequence of drain,
remove and destroy is therefore safe from any thread, but that thread
must not itself run one of the callbacks of this registration.

It is safe to call this function more than one time on the same reg,
both for calls one after another and for calls that truly run at the
same time on different threads. The reg handle also stays safe to pass
to a later ccol_event_loop_modify(), ccol_event_loop_pause()/_resume(),
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation() or ccol_event_loop_remove() call
after a removal. The library resolves reg against the registration
table of its own loop before it dereferences anything, so it always
finds a handle that is stale, or that something already removed, and
never risks a use-after-free.

The two repeat-call cases return different values. A call that runs
strictly after an earlier ccol_event_loop_remove() on the same reg
returned finds that reg_h does not resolve at all, because that
earlier call released the slot of reg before it returned, so it gets
ccol_invalid_args. That is the same outcome that
ccol_event_loop_modify(), _pause(), _resume() and
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation() give for a reg that something
removed. A call that truly races another ccol_event_loop_remove() on
the same reg is different: its own resolve can succeed, in a narrow
window where the other call has not yet released the slot of reg, and
it then sees that reg is already marked as removed, returns
ccol_success and does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop the registration belongs to.
reg   Registration to remove.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The function removed the registration, or
                    another call on the same reg that truly runs at
                    the same time removed it first (see
                    DESCRIPTION).
ccol_invalid_args   loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, or a handle that
                    is stale, or one that something already destroyed;
                    or reg is CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, or it fails to
                    resolve for another reason, such as an earlier
                    ccol_event_loop_remove() call that has returned
                    and has removed it.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from any thread, including at the same time as a dispatch
for the same registration and from inside the callback of that
registration.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void on_readable(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg,
                 ccol_selectable *sel, void *arg) {
    conn_ctx_t *ctx = (conn_ctx_t *)arg;
    char buf[512];
    ssize_t n = read(sel->fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
    if (n <= 0) {
        ccol_event_loop_remove(loop, reg);  /* self-removal, safe */
        close(sel->fd);
        free(ctx);
        return;
    }
    /* ... */
}
.fi
